  "auth_observations": [
    "Amtrak first-party requests rely heavily on cookies (Akamai bot-mitigation cookies like bm_sz, _abck, ak_bmsc; plus Adobe/Optanon consent cookies).",
    "CSRF token endpoint exists at /libs/granite/csrf/token.json (AEM Granite). In the captured journey search POST, the request header shows `CSRF-Token: undefined`, suggesting CSRF may be optional for that endpoint or set via JS in other flows.",
    "No Authorization: Bearer tokens or API keys were observed in inspected first-party API calls; session appears primarily cookie-based.",
    "Bot mitigation is prominent: frequent POSTs to an opaque /sPu4u-zM/... path and /akam/ pixel endpoints (Akamai).",
    "reCAPTCHA Enterprise traffic is present (anchor GET and /ccm/form-data POSTs to google.com)."
  ],
  "narrative": "Capture contains 419 total requests across 15 hosts; a large majority are analytics/telemetry (Decibel Insight 101 reqs, Adobe Analytics smetrics 22, Branch.io 12, multiple ad pixels) plus bot-mitigation (Akamai sensor posts) and chat (Nuance). The core functional Amtrak endpoints relevant to automation are concentrated on www.amtrak.com and include a primary action endpoint `POST /dotcom/journey-solution-option` for journey search/solutions and several supporting data feeds for stations and reference data (stations info, station mappings, routes list, static fare rules). First-party API responses are JSON and appear to be served from an AEM/dispatcher-style content path (/content/amtrak/...) and /services/* JSON endpoints. Auth/session handling appears cookie-driven with CSRF token support available via Granite; no bearer-token style API auth was evident in the inspected calls. Noise requests include numerous trackers/pixels and security telemetry; these should be excluded from automation except where bot/recaptcha challenges must be handled."
}